# Minimize Nesting

Prefer the shape that minimizes the **sum of indentation levels over all
lines** in a function (or contiguous block). Deep nests for the common path
are worse than flat early exits for the rare paths.

Typical move: invert the condition and **return early** (or continue) so the
main logic sits at lower indent.

```cpp
// BAD — common path buried; high indent sum
if(const val::clo* closure = std::get_if<val::clo>(&f.slot->content)) {
    const expr* term = closure->term;
    if(std::get_if<expr::abs>(&term->content) != nullptr)
        return std::nullopt;
    // …more nested work…
}
if(std::get_if<val::fvar>(&f.slot->content) != nullptr)
    return std::nullopt;
return std::nullopt;

// GOOD — bail out of the odd cases; main path stays shallow
if(std::get_if<val::fvar>(&f.slot->content) != nullptr)
    return std::nullopt;
if(std::holds_alternative<val::napp>(f.slot->content))
    return std::nullopt;
auto& closure = std::get<val::clo>(f.slot->content);
const expr* term = closure.term;
if(std::get_if<expr::abs>(&term->content) != nullptr)
    return std::nullopt;
// …main work at low indent…
```

Do not add abstraction just to “flatten” indent. Prefer guard clauses and
inverted conditions first.
